              "id": "F1",
              "kind": "claimed_speedup",
              "family": "Valid tags with many dot-separated components in each of the three fields (interpreters, ABIs, platforms). The new implementation splits each component only once, while the old version split each component three times.",
              "why": "The new version builds component_parts with a single split per component and reuses the lists for validation and the Cartesian product, avoiding repeated splits.",
              "size_meaning": "n controls the number of dot-separated items per component; total tags generated = n^3, and string length grows ~ O(n).",
              "make_source": "\ndef make(n):\n    # Generate n interpreters, n abis, n platforms\n    interpreters = '.'.join(f'py{i}' for i in range(2, 2 + n))\n    abis = '.'.join(f'abi{i}' for i in range(n))\n    platforms = '.'.join(f'plat{i}' for i in range(n))\n    tag = f'{interpreters}-{abis}-{platforms}'\n    return [[tag]]",
              "example_args": [],
              "origin": "plan",
              "screen_ratio": 0.9510078429461927,
              "valid_fraction": 1.0,
              "error": null
            },
            {
              "id": "F2",
              "kind": "suspected_slowdown",
              "family": "invalid",
              "why": "The new implementation splits all three \"-\" components up‑front (building a list of three split lists) before checking for empty components. The old code checks components one‑by‑one and aborts on the first empty component, splitting only the components examined so far. For an invalid tag whose first component contains an empty \".\" segment while the other two components are very large (many \"..\" segments), the new version does three large splits before raising, whereas the old version does only one split before raising, making the new version slower on this invalid input family.",
              "size_meaning": "n controls the number of dot‑segments in the large second and third components (the first component always contains a single empty segment). Cost grows roughly linearly with n because splitting a long string is O(length).",
              "make_source": "\ndef make(n):\n    # Produce up to 8 test cases, growing the size of the large components.\n    out = []\n    for i in range(1, min(n, 8) + 1):\n        # First component: a short prefix, then an empty segment, then a short suffix.\n        first = \".\".join([\"a\"] * i + [\"\"] + [\"b\"] * i)\n        # Second and third components: many dot‑segments to make splitting expensive.\n        second = \".\".join([\"c\"] * (i * 20))\n        third = \".\".join([\"d\"] * (i * 20))\n        tag = \"-\".join([first, second, third])\n        out.append([tag])          # parse_tag takes a single positional string argument\n    return out",
